00:15:20.130 --> 00:15:24.420
of the things that cause us to be hungry
or full. You may also have a better time

00:15:24.420 --> 00:15:28.440
trusting your body and actually feeling
your fullness. Here are some factors that

00:15:28.440 --> 00:15:33.690
may affect your fullness levels. Number
one - time how much time has passed as the

00:15:33.690 --> 00:15:38.040
last time you ate. It sounds obvious, but
the more frequently you eat the less

00:15:38.040 --> 00:15:42.180
hungry you end up being. Going too long
between your meals can cause your blood

00:15:42.180 --> 00:15:47.250
sugar levels to plummet, your hunger
hormone skyrocketing, and you end up

00:15:47.250 --> 00:15:51.300
overeating. If you know that your lunch
and dinner are going to be very far

00:15:51.300 --> 00:15:55.170
apart, this is when you'd want to
incorporate some snacks in between.

00:15:55.170 --> 00:15:59.310
This allows you to fuel your body and brain
and prevents you from experiencing that

00:15:59.310 --> 00:16:05.070
intense hunger. And again, I know this
will be a question "But Abbey, snacking

00:16:05.070 --> 00:16:11.280
isn't healthy" and this leads me to my
second point - types of food. Despite

00:16:11.280 --> 00:16:16.800
popular belief, snacks are not inherently
unhealthy. But, of course, we can always

00:16:16.800 --> 00:16:21.170
make more satiating nutritious choice. So
a snack that is high in healthy fats,

00:16:21.170 --> 00:16:26.370
protein, and fiber, will help keep you
full much longer than ones that are just

00:16:26.370 --> 00:16:31.020
high in simple sugars. Simple sugars give
you a quick energy boost which is great

00:16:31.020 --> 00:16:35.580
certain situations like a high intensity
workout. But without any fiber, protein, or

00:16:35.580 --> 00:16:40.170
good fats, it can often be followed by a
rapid crash in blood sugar levels.

00:16:40.170 --> 00:16:43.920
This can lead you to feel hungry really soon
after. So if you're noticing that you're

00:16:43.920 --> 00:16:48.270
hungry after a snack or meal check in to
see what that meal or snack consisted of.

00:16:48.270 --> 00:16:52.620
Ideally you want a meal to have a good
source of protein, fiber from some whole

00:16:52.620 --> 00:16:56.610
grains or veggies, and some healthy fats.
I call this the hunger crushing

00:16:56.610 --> 00:17:01.290
combination and together these compounds take longer to digest, keeping you

00:17:01.290 --> 00:17:04.890
feeling full much longer.
Number three - social influences.

00:17:04.890 --> 00:17:09.060
Interestingly, we eat differently
depending on who is around us. In fact,

00:17:09.060 --> 00:17:12.839
research suggests that we tend to eat
more food and for much longer when we

00:17:12.839 --> 00:17:16.770
eat in large groups. And this makes sense,
if we're by ourselves

00:17:16.770 --> 00:17:22.980
we usually just eat our food and move on,
but remember food is a social activity.

00:17:22.980 --> 00:17:27.689
So of course, we spend time eating and
chatting and doing all those fun things

00:17:27.689 --> 00:17:31.470
when we're with our friends. However,
research suggests that some dieters are

00:17:31.470 --> 00:17:35.160
more likely to be influenced by the food
choices of others around them and may

00:17:35.160 --> 00:17:39.960
purposefully under eat out of fear being
judged, especially in the presence of

00:17:39.960 --> 00:17:45.450
"model eaters". In either situation, we tend
to ignore our biological cues for hunger

00:17:45.450 --> 00:17:47.780
and fullness when in front of other people.

00:17:47.780 --> 00:17:52.100
My suggestion is to remind yourself that no
one knows your body's needs like you do.

00:17:52.100 --> 00:17:56.600
If your friend is having a salad or
barely touches their meal, it may be it's

00:17:56.600 --> 00:18:00.530
because they had a big pancake breakfast,
or if another friend is having a burger

00:18:00.530 --> 00:18:05.240
and fries or polishes off their plate
and pressures you to do the same, you

00:18:05.240 --> 00:18:08.770
never know they maybe skipped breakfast
and are really hungry for a hearty meal.

00:18:08.770 --> 00:18:14.000
Everyone's needs are different, so choose
a meal that satisfies your appetite and

00:18:14.000 --> 00:18:17.600
make a point to check in with yourself
throughout the meal. Use the social

00:18:17.600 --> 00:18:22.370
activity as an opportunity to really
slow down between bites to engage in

00:18:22.370 --> 00:18:25.610
conversation and to check in with your
hunger and fullness throughout the meal.

00:18:25.610 --> 00:18:31.580
Next, let's talk about "air food". Now a lot
of diet foods are full of air I mean no

00:18:31.580 --> 00:18:36.169
literally like a rice cake is full of
air. Hence why I say it's a vessel for

00:18:36.169 --> 00:18:39.049
peanut butter in my house but, hey, that's
just me. Now the reason why these

00:18:39.049 --> 00:18:43.010
foods are not necessarily the best
snacks on their own is because they only

00:18:43.010 --> 00:18:47.480
temporarily decrease hunger. The food
enters your stomach and kind of tricks

00:18:47.480 --> 00:18:51.530
you into thinking you're full because it
adds volume but because it's lacking any

00:18:51.530 --> 00:18:56.540
real sustenance, you'll be really hungry
pretty quickly. This also can backfire

00:18:56.540 --> 00:19:00.380
because these foods tend to have a
health "halo effect". You know that they're

00:19:00.380 --> 00:19:04.460
low in calories, so you can eat a large
portion of them to fill your stomach up

00:19:04.460 --> 00:19:09.440
but at the end of the day you could have
become full off of a nutrient dense meal

00:19:09.440 --> 00:19:14.120
and stayed fuller a lot longer. So a lot
of people, for example, turn to salads as

00:19:14.120 --> 00:19:19.460
a healthy option but these too can be
air food, as well. So lettuce in a bowl by

00:19:19.460 --> 00:19:25.010
itself is by no means a salad. So here
are some ways to up the staying power of

00:19:25.010 --> 00:19:29.750
your salad. First is to add protein, so
things like shredded chicken, tuna,

00:19:29.750 --> 00:19:35.600
chickpeas, kidney beans, lentils, and tofu,
are all great options. Also add some

00:19:35.600 --> 00:19:40.220
carbs - quinoa, couscous, or have some
whole-grain crackers or bread on the

00:19:40.220 --> 00:19:45.919
side. Don't forget about fat as well, so
olive oil, avocado, cheese, nuts, seeds, etc.

00:19:45.919 --> 00:19:50.900
Now whether we're talking a salad, or
another meal or snack, the goal here is

00:19:50.900 --> 00:19:55.610
to incorporate slow-digesting
macronutrients. So fat, protein, and fibre

00:19:55.610 --> 00:19:59.240
rich carbs. Again, we will talk way more
about this in the episode

00:19:59.240 --> 00:20:04.340
on gentle nutrition. But bottom line,
becoming aware of fullness factors can

00:20:04.340 --> 00:20:07.640
really help your inner food
anthropologist determine the reasons for

00:20:07.640 --> 00:20:11.360
your quickly returning hunger. Once
you've determined the cause, you can

00:20:11.360 --> 00:20:14.570
slowly incorporate some intuitive eating
principles to work towards feeling your

00:20:14.570 --> 00:20:18.740
fullness for much longer. If you feel
like you can't stop eating, even if

00:20:18.740 --> 00:20:22.790
you're full, then this may be a sign that
you're using food as a coping mechanism.

00:20:22.790 --> 00:20:26.840
I will be diving into this a lot more when
we discuss principle number seven, so

00:20:26.840 --> 00:20:30.890
definitely stay tuned for that. Now, your
homework for this week. So this week, I

00:20:30.890 --> 00:20:34.850
really want you to focus on eating
consciously. Like I mentioned earlier, I

00:20:34.850 --> 00:20:39.230
want you to take a pause a few times
while you're eating really focus on the

00:20:39.230 --> 00:20:43.700
flavors of the food, your hunger and
fullness levels, and take charge in

00:20:43.700 --> 00:20:48.290
deciding when you're done eating. Find
yourself a visual cue that you can use

00:20:48.290 --> 00:20:51.950
to help signal to yourself that you're
done, and be sure to rate your fullness

00:20:51.950 --> 00:20:56.120
using that satiety scale. Again, a
reminder that there is no right or wrong

00:20:56.120 --> 00:21:00.590
with hunger-fullness scale, just take
notice of where you land before a meal,

00:21:00.590 --> 00:21:06.230
during a meal, and after a meal. Jot down
how it makes you feel, what foods you ate,

00:21:06.230 --> 00:21:10.040
and how well they helped your hunger.
Continue to work on these steps

00:21:10.040 --> 00:21:13.400
throughout your intuitive eating journey,
and of course stay tuned for our next

00:21:13.400 --> 00:21:17.870
video on principle number six -
discover the satisfaction factor.  So

00:21:17.870 --> 00:21:22.250
folks, that is all for today. If you liked
it be sure to give it a thumbs up, leave

00:21:22.250 --> 00:21:24.920
me a comment below if you have any
questions about intuitive eating,

00:21:24.920 --> 00:21:30.880
subscribe to the channel, and I will see
you next time on Abbey's Kitchen. Bye!

